            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I was looking around at how the callback merging works, and noticed that it can actually accept a table instead of a function ([code](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/5885a30e1e1225349079e7a1c4a3848aa8e43e42/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/client.lua#L1179), available since v0.10). Note that list (table with contiguous indices) are merged as atoms. So, relying on the merging rule,  the callbacks are merged as follows (where other can be either function or list per `vim.islist`):\n\n| Value   | Override | Result |\n| ---------- | ------------ | --------- |\n| keyed_table_a | keyed_table_b | merged tables |\n|other_a | keyed_table_b | keyed_table_b\n|keyed_table_a | other_b |  other_b\n|other_a | other_b | other_b\n\nSo, wouldn't it be better to define the `on_attach`'s in the `lsp` directory as follows?\n```lua\n{\n    on_attach = {\n        default = function(client, bufnr)\n        end\n    },\n}\n```\nor \n```lua\n{\n    on_attach = {\n        lspconfig = function(client, bufnr)\n        end\n    },\n}\n```\n\nThis would allow the user of the package to extend / replace the on_attach as seem fit.\n",
            "created_at": "2026-09-15T08:28:14Z",
